Re-derive the SGI VC2 video-timing table when its RAM is written, not only when
register 0x00 is written.

STATUS (2026-08-02): this corrects a REAL emulation inaccuracy, but it does NOT
fix the intermittent black-screen boot hang it was written for, and it is NOT in
the production build. Read both sections below before using it.

THE INACCURACY (proven, and what this patch fixes)

A real VC2 re-reads its video-timing table out of its own RAM continuously, on
every frame. MAME instead DECODES that table once, in
`vc2_device::update_screen_size()`, and calls it from exactly one place: a write
to VC2 register 0x00 (video entry pointer). Instrumenting a cold boot (log the
emulated timestamp of every register-0x00 write and count the RAM words written
between them):

    2.785  REG0 vid=0400 ramw=150   readout=386,41..1674,1065 size=1288x1024
    3.586  REG0 vid=0400 ramw=1455  readout=386,41..1674,1065 size=1288x1024
   10.865  REG0 vid=0400 ramw=2760  readout=386,41..1674,1065 size=1288x1024
   57.830  REG0 vid=0400 ramw=4066  readout=386,41..1674,1065 size=1288x1024
   59.510  RAMW n=7408 addr=1000
   61.940  RAMW n=8564 addr=0500

The last register-0x00 write is at emulated t=57.8 s; IRIX then writes ~4,500
further timing-RAM words and never touches register 0x00 again. Every frame for
the rest of the session is scanned out from a decode of a table that no longer
exists. That is a genuine divergence from the hardware and worth fixing on its
own merits.

WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX (measured, do not re-litigate)

The hypothesis was that a stale decode would sometimes leave a DEGENERATE
rectangle -- `set_size()` blanking the screen with no way back -- and that this
was the intermittent black-screen boot hang. That is wrong:

  * A captured hang shows the emulated screen geometry reading 1288x1024 for
    every one of 180 samples, straight through the blackout. The screen is black
    while set_size() is perfectly healthy.
  * In a 60-trial controlled A/B (control and treatment built from ONE tree,
    differing only in this patch) the treatment cell hung anyway, with the
    identical signature: black at emulated t=59, geometry constant, emulated
    clock still advancing.

So the blackness lives elsewhere -- framebuffer contents, the CMAP palette, the
XMAP mode entries, or the guest's X server -- and the mitigation that actually
works is the boot watchdog in `streamhost/tiles/irix/x11-runtime.sh`.

THE CHANGE

Mark the table dirty on any write to the timing RAM and re-derive it at vblank,
which is what the hardware effectively does. Two details keep it cheap and
behaviour-preserving:

  * Throttled to one re-decode per 8 vblanks, because `decode_vt_table()` clears
    a 16 MB scratch table. The dirty flag is only ever set while software is
    actively programming the timing RAM (four short bursts in a whole IRIX
    boot), so the steady-state cost is ZERO re-decodes.
  * `update_screen_size()` now signals `m_screen_timing_changed` only when the
    derived rectangle actually MOVED, so a routine that previously ran ~4 times
    per boot does not reconfigure the screen every frame during a mode set.

Both new members are `save_item()`-registered. Measured cost: nil -- paired
trials of control vs patched reached the same emulated time in 272 s vs 274 s.

Apply from the MAME source root:
  patch -p1 -d src/devices/bus/gio64 < mame-newport-vc2-restale-timing.patch

--- a/newport.cpp	2026-07-31 11:44:24.501411689 +0300
+++ b/newport.cpp	2026-07-31 11:44:24.526067872 +0300
@@ -339,6 +339,9 @@
 	save_item(NAME(m_readout_y1));
 
 	save_item(NAME(m_enable_cursor));
+
+	save_item(NAME(m_vt_dirty));
+	save_item(NAME(m_vt_dirty_wait));
 }
 
 void vc2_device::device_reset()
@@ -372,6 +375,9 @@
 	m_readout_x1 = 0;
 	m_readout_y1 = 0;
 
+	m_vt_dirty = false;
+	m_vt_dirty_wait = 0;
+
 	m_enable_cursor = false;
 }
 
@@ -456,6 +462,7 @@
 		if (offset == 3)
 		{
 			LOGMASKED(LOG_VC2, "RAM Write: %04x = %08x\n", m_ram_addr, (uint16_t)data);
+			m_vt_dirty = true;
 			m_ram[m_ram_addr] = (uint16_t)data;
 			m_ram_addr++;
 			if (m_ram_addr >= RAM_SIZE)
@@ -723,6 +730,11 @@
 
 void vc2_device::update_screen_size()
 {
+	const int old_x0 = m_readout_x0;
+	const int old_y0 = m_readout_y0;
+	const int old_x1 = m_readout_x1;
+	const int old_y1 = m_readout_y1;
+
 	decode_vt_table();
 
 	bool x_started = false;
@@ -771,7 +783,15 @@
 		}
 	}
 
-	m_screen_timing_changed(1);
+	// Only disturb the screen when the derived geometry actually moved: this
+	// now runs whenever the timing RAM is written, not just on a register-0x00
+	// write, and re-configuring the screen every frame would be both wasteful
+	// and visible.
+	if (m_readout_x0 != old_x0 || m_readout_y0 != old_y0
+		|| m_readout_x1 != old_x1 || m_readout_y1 != old_y1)
+	{
+		m_screen_timing_changed(1);
+	}
 }
 
